# .scrubignore - paths the leak scanner reports as NOTE instead of LEAK.
#
# Why this file exists: a scanner that reports 218 findings of which 3 are real is a
# scanner nobody reads. Everything listed here has been reviewed once by hand; it is
# third-party code vendored with its own test fixtures and its own authors' contact
# details, published by those authors. Findings there are still COUNTED and printed as
# "note" lines - they are never silently dropped.
#
# Format: one path prefix or glob per line, forward slashes, relative to the repo root.
# A line matches if the file path starts with it OR matches it as a glob.
#
# Rule for adding a line: only a directory you did not write, whose findings you have
# actually read. Never add one of your own files to make the scan pass.
#
# Keep this list IN SYNC with the paths block of .gitleaks.toml. gitleaks reads its own
# config, not this file, so the same decision has to be written twice - and the CI job
# goes red the moment they disagree.

# Vendored dev-workflow toolkit: ~200 test fixtures using test@test.com / git@github.com
skills/gstack/

# Third-party skills that ship their author's own public contact in their README
skills/huashu-design/
skills/html-ppt/

# Upstream API reference with doc examples (test@test.com, /Users/me)
skills/playwright-skill/

# The owner's own training libraries: course transcripts, supplier and prospect addresses,
# his own shop's contact address. This is the material the private repo exists to carry.
# Demoting it changes NOTHING about credentials - a Shopify/Anthropic/GitHub/AWS token
# under this path still blocks the commit. Only PII is demoted here.
formations/
